      "writtenOn": "2021-02-19T20:24:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Huh, interesting. I\u0027m not that well versed on HID descriptors, and the documentation seems fuzzy on this, but I did find what appears to be an example of a keyboard descriptor that uses multiple usage ranges in section E.6 of the HID 1.11 spec (https://www.usb.org/sites/default/files/hid1_11.pdf, page 79 going by the PDF page numbers or 69 by the on-page numbers). Maybe we have put each range in a separate \"report\" in the same collection?",
      "parentUuid": "2b37af22_51b76dd9",
      "revId": "6119579ffd4ba70656d4f501d1b0d44658f408da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ca142d72_c71452cd",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/usb_hid_keyboard.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 190,
      "author": {
        "id": 1141739
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-23T11:12:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m new to this too.\nYeah it\u0027s possible to split the keys array into three parts: 6 byte for key 0x00~0x48, one byte (bit field) for arrow keys, and another 6 bytes for key 0x64~0x98.\nThis requires non-trivial change to split the existing logic into boot protocol and report protocol handler (we have to keep compatible with boot protocol). I will try to make a POC first and see if it works.",
      "parentUuid": "a5cdf100_ac86a29c",
      "revId": "6119579ffd4ba70656d4f501d1b0d44658f408da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
